        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-310179",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(3{x}^{4}-5)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061u,\\,u=3{x}^{4}-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={sec}^{2}\u2061u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}\u2061u\\cdot 12{x}^{3} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}\u2061u \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}\u2061(3{x}^{4}-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      "knowledge_point": {
        "title": "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"
      },
